+# CR-033 — The date of Noe Ward's death
+
+**Status:** APPROVED — direct author decision, 26 October 2026. **Amends:** the date in [CR-021](CR-021.md) F1 and revision-9 decision R9-09. **Scope:** only when Noe dies. Every other part of F1, R9-09 and CR-027 A20 keeps its existing scope.
+
+## Author decision
+
+> I've decided Noe dies in spring 1889, not autumn 1888. Keep the typhoid account, the poisoning testimony and the unsolved mystery exactly as they are. Integrate this into canon now — master storyline, CR register, character docs, whatever it touches — and run the sync and validators.
+
+## Operative change
+
+Kaʻanoe (Noe) Ward dies in **spring 1889**, during a typhoid outbreak she is fighting. This replaces "autumn 1888" wherever current canon dates her death.
+
+These parts stay exactly as they were:
+
+- **Official account:** typhoid, on a certificate signed by Victoria's physician.
+- **Testimony:** her servants swear she was poisoned.
+- **Registers:** they disappear for a month and reappear in the Office of Public Measures.
+- **Rumours:** loyalists call it murder, and Silas's people spread the story.
+- **Culpability:** several parties have motives, but no culprit is established and the Chronicler can prove none guilty.
+
+Nothing in this decision makes any suspect more or less likely.
+
+Her identity, medical career, 1886 hānai bargain, health board and limited achievement in registers and vaccination are unchanged. Julian's 1886 birth and adoption, Elias's August 1887 death, the 1887 regency, Donner in October 1887 and the 1888 Office of Public Measures are also unchanged.
+
+## Consequential continuity
+
+- **The "Regent's year" rumour.** The storyline lists three deaths: Caleb, the Donner telegraphist "burned in his hut that January", and Noe. Once Noe dies in 1889, "that January" would silently move the telegraphist's death to January 1889. The storyline now says January 1888, which keeps his existing date. The rumour now spans about eighteen months, from October 1887 to spring 1889. Whether it keeps the name "the Regent's year" is **[OPEN-033-02]**. No replacement name is invented.
+- **Her working span.** Her health-board work now runs from 1886 to spring 1889 rather than 1886–1888. That is still under three years, so "she does not cure the islands in three years" still holds.
+- **Overlaps with existing 1888 events.** Several events now fall while she is alive and directing the health board:
+  - the founding of the Office of Public Measures in 1888;
+  - the start of Victoria's island-wide vaccination programme (1888–1892);
+  - the proposed (CR-027) Hollerith tabulation of her registers from 1888.
+
+  These dates are left unchanged, and nothing new is invented about how her work relates to them. How these events relate to her work before she dies is **[OPEN-033-01]**.
+- **Julian's age.** Julian, born 1886, is about two or three when she dies, rather than about two. No scene detail depends on the difference.
+
+## Not changed
+
+- The first-draft manuscripts, the original 250-profile `character_bible.json`, archived CR attachments and outputs packages are not changed. Neither are the earlier reading records that quote "autumn 1888" (the CR-021 source chronology and F1 text, and the decision lists reproduced in CR-022, CR-026, CR-028, CR-030 and dependency_decisions_211026.md). CR-033 takes precedence over them on the date only.
+- The CR-032 alternatives do not date Noe's death and are not modified.
+- No financial, demographic or MODEL figure depends on this date. The workbook is unchanged.
