+**Julian and Noe Ward.** Kaʻanoe Ward's medical examination, registers and hānai bargain give her agency independent of the men and dynasty. Her spring 1889 death has an official typhoid account and incompatible testimony, with no proven culprit. Julian inherits her Hawaiian claim and Victoria's political training, becomes Kamehameha VI in Hawaii and Sovereign of Pacifica, and uses personal rule without the title President. His friendship with Hirohito is real; he withholds the Altai secret. In the regency's sealed papers he finds that the mother who raised him kept the Donner lie, and that Noe's death had more suspects than he knew.
 
 **The Japanese partners.** Manjirō and the early reformers establish cooperation; Kuroda Kiyotaka supplies Kiyoshi’s adopted house; Takahashi links Tokyo’s finance to San Francisco and later union. Japan’s crown, cabinets and Diet remain real centers of domestic authority. Imperial careers attract the navy and commercial elites, while the national army’s corridor demands create a lasting opposition.
 
